devto_markdown:
High-throughput network parsers (Kestrel, custom TCP, Kafka consumers) read wire data as `ReadOnlySpan<byte>`. Historically, running a clean `switch` statement on headers or commands required either string transcoding with `Encoding.UTF8.GetString` or chaining verbose `SequenceEqual("...u8)` checks.

### Before
```csharp
// Allocates a string on Gen0 for every wire message
var method = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(buffer);
return method switch {
    "GET" => HttpMethod.Get,
    "POST" => HttpMethod.Post,
    _ => HttpMethod.Custom
};
```

### After
```csharp
// C# 15 / .NET 11: Direct switch pattern on byte spans
return buffer switch {
    "GET"u8 => HttpMethod.Get,
    "POST"u8 => HttpMethod.Post,
    _ => HttpMethod.Custom
};
```

### Why it matters
• **Zero Allocations:** Completely removes Gen0 garbage collection pressure inside hot loop parser paths.
• **Jump Table Optimization:** The JIT compiler turns these literal comparisons into tight branch tables and SIMD sequence checks without intermediate pointer overhead.
• **Readability:** Replaces verbose `if (buffer.SequenceEqual("GET"u8))` chains with idiomatic pattern matching syntax.

Watch out: Span pattern matching evaluates ordinal byte equivalence. If your wire protocol is case-insensitive, you must canonicalize the slice into a scratch span first.

